To connect your local Git repository to GitHub using gh auth login, open your PDF, and transfer files between your phone and computer, follow these simple steps:
1. Authenticate GitHub CLI (gh) with Your Token
Instead of using a web browser, log in directly using your personal access token (PAT):
 * Run the login command:
   gh auth login

 * When prompted:
   * Select GitHub.com.
   * Select HTTPS (or SSH if configured).
   * Select Paste an authentication token when asked how you want to authenticate.
 * Paste your GitHub token into the terminal and press Enter.
2. Opening PDF Files in Linux Terminal (xdg-open)
To open files (like PDFs or images) from your Linux terminal using your system's default viewer, use xdg-open:
xdg-open Linux_Git_Cheat_Sheet.pdf

3. Transferring a PDF from Your Phone to Your Computer
Choose the method that works best for your setup:
 * USB Cable (MTP - Fast & Direct):
   * Connect your phone to your Linux PC via USB.
   * On your phone, tap the USB notification and select File Transfer / MTP.
   * Open your file manager on Linux, navigate to your phone's storage, and copy the PDF into ~/Desktop/linux_practice.
 * Local Network Transfer (KDE Connect / LocalSend):
   * Install LocalSend or KDE Connect on both your phone and PC.
   * Connect both devices to the same Wi-Fi network and send the file directly across devices.
 * Cloud Storage (Google Drive / Email):
   * Upload the PDF from your phone to Google Drive or send it via email/messaging.
   * Open your web browser on Linux and download it directly into your project directory.

